│ patch_description        │ Replace the exponentially-weighted moving average (which introduces lag and still allows  │
│                          │ noise-driven slope reversals) with a zero-phase local polynomial (Savitzky-Golay style)   │
│                          │ filter. A degree-2 polynomial fit over each sliding window passes through the data with   │
│                          │ no phase delay, preserves linear and quadratic trends (greatly reducing lag error and     │
│                          │ slope changes), and its least-squares nature suppresses noise more effectively than a     │
│                          │ weighted mean, reducing false reversals. Centering the fit on the window means no         │
│                          │ directional bias toward past samples, so responsiveness is maintained. This addresses all │
│                          │ four objectives: slope-change minimization, lag minimization, tracking accuracy, and      │
│                          │ false-reversal avoidance, while keeping O(W) work per sample (efficient). We also make    │
│                          │ the basic filter use the same approach so both code paths benefit.                        │
