│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ robust_weighted_trend_regression                                                          │
│ patch_description        │ The current enhanced filter is a plain exponentially-weighted moving average. A weighted  │
│                          │ moving average is biased whenever the signal has a slope: it lags behind trends, which    │
│                          │ hurts lag_error, accuracy, and tracking. A better approach is a *weighted local linear    │
│                          │ (degree-1) regression* over each window, evaluated at the most recent sample (the window  │
│                          │ end). This is zero-lag with respect to linear trends: fitting y ≈ a·t + b over the window │
│                          │ and evaluating at the last sample removes the systematic trend-induced bias of a moving   │
│                          │ average, while the exponential weights still emphasize recent samples for adaptivity to   │
│                          │ non-stationarity. Additionally, a single Huber-style robust reweighting pass downweights  │
│                          │ outlier samples within the window, suppressing noise spikes that cause spurious           │
│                          │ directional reversals (improving slope_changes and false_reversals). Solving the          │
│                          │ 2-parameter weighted least squares in closed form is cheap (O(W) per window), preserving  │
│                          │ the excellent efficiency score. To avoid amplifying noise when the residual scale is tiny │
│                          │ or the system is ill-conditioned, we fall back gracefully to the weighted mean.           │
