│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ adaptive_kalman_trend_filter                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ Instead of a sliding-window weighted average (which has fixed lag proportional to window  │
│                          │ size), I use a causal adaptive Kalman filter with a local linear trend state model        │
│                          │ (position + slope). The Kalman filter jointly estimates the level and slope of the        │
│                          │ signal, with online noise estimation: the measurement noise R is adapted from recent      │
│                          │ innovation statistics, and the process noise Q is adapted based on detected trend changes │
│                          │ (innovation whiteness test). This gives near-zero phase lag (unlike windowed averages     │
│                          │ which lag by ~(W-1)/2), suppresses spurious slope reversals (the slope state acts as a    │
│                          │ regularized derivative that resists noise), and preserves genuine dynamics (adaptive Q    │
│                          │ lets the filter respond quickly to real trend changes). The output is then aligned to     │
│                          │ match the original sliding-window output length. This directly optimizes all four         │
│                          │ objectives: slope change minimization, lag minimization, tracking accuracy, and false     │
│                          │ reversal avoidance.                                                                       │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
