│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ lag_comp_and_blend_reversal_fix                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ Two targeted improvements to the enhanced filter:                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Lag compensation via slope forecasting.** The Holt smoother introduces a systematic  │
│                          │ phase delay (lag_error = 0.42 hurts responsiveness). Since the Kalman state already       │
│                          │ carries a slope estimate, we can explicitly compensate the group delay by extrapolating   │
│                          │ each sample forward by a small number of steps: y = hs + lag_k * slope. This directly     │
│                          │ reduces lag error and average error without additional smoothing (which would destroy     │
│                          │ dynamics). The lag constant (2.0 samples) is small and safe — it shifts the smoother's    │
│                          │ phase forward without amplifying high-frequency noise, since the slope estimate itself is │
│                          │ already Kalman-smoothed.                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Blend instead of full slope-reintegration in Stage 3.** Currently the output is      │
│                          │ *entirely* rebuilt by re-integrating the median-filtered slopes. Pure reintegration is an │
│                          │ integrator: any small bias in the gated slope sequence accumulates as unbounded drift,    │
│                          │ decoupling the output from the actual signal levels (visible in avg_error = 0.47 and      │
│                          │ correlation dropping 0.84 → 0.83 despite fewer slope changes). A convex blend (0.7 of the │
│                          │ level-anchored Holt output + 0.3 of the slope-reintegrated output) keeps most of the      │
│                          │ false-reversal suppression (slope_changes = 103, false_reversals = 85.4 are already much  │
│                          │ better than baseline) while eliminating the drift: the level-anchored component acts as   │
│                          │ an anchor/low-frequency corrector for the integrator. This trades a small amount of       │
│                          │ reversal suppression for a large gain in tracking accuracy and lag/avg error.             │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Both changes preserve the window-alignment contract (`y_full`) and all function           │
│                          │ signatures, so the rest of the program is untouched.                                      │
