│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ zlag_robls_trendlock                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ The baseline uses a plain or exponentially-weighted moving average, which induces a lag   │
│                          │ of roughly (W-1)/2 samples and flattens genuine trends. Key insights drawn from the       │
│                          │ context: (1) MA lag is the dominant cause of poor lag_error and false reversals; (2)      │
│                          │ weighted windows help noise but still lag. Novel approach: replace averaging with a       │
│                          │ per-window robust weighted polynomial (local quadratic) least-squares fit evaluated at    │
│                          │ the *leading edge* of the window — this extrapolates away the group delay analytically,   │
│                          │ giving near-zero phase lag. Robustness is added via iteratively reweighted least squares  │
│                          │ (Huber weights) so noise spikes don't drag the estimate. Finally, a lightweight           │
│                          │ "trend-lock" step damps spurious slope reversals: the local slope estimate is smoothed    │
│                          │ with a shrinkage factor that only weakens when evidence is strong, minimizing slope       │
│                          │ changes and false reversals while preserving true trend turning points. All processing    │
│                          │ remains causal-per-window with O(W) work per step, keeping efficiency near 1.0.           │
