│ patch_description        │ The current pipeline's final stage re-weights already-smoothed data with a Python loop,   │
│                          │ adding both compute cost and residual group delay (the exponential weights emphasize the  │
│                          │ window start-relative position, shifting the effective evaluation point backward and      │
│                          │ inflating lag_error to 0.41). This edit (1) replaces the per-sample loop with a single    │
│                          │ `np.convolve` using a precomputed weight vector (identical math, O(n) instead of O(n·W)   │
│                          │ Python overhead), and (2) spends the recovered efficiency on a light inter-window         │
│                          │ slope-consistency pass: we estimate noise σ robustly via MAD of the input's first         │
│                          │ differences, and wherever consecutive filtered values differ by less than 0.5σ — i.e.,    │
│                          │ the difference is statistically indistinguishable from noise and cannot represent a       │
│                          │ genuine trend change — we snap the sample toward a 5-tap running median blend. This       │
│                          │ directly attacks slope_changes (41.2) and false_reversals (36.6) without touching genuine │
│                          │ dynamics, since real trend changes exceed the noise floor. The smoothing kernel is also   │
│                          │ rebalanced toward the window's later samples to shave a bit of phase lag, improving       │
│                          │ responsiveness_score and correlation.                                                     │
