├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ anchor_reintegration_and_lag_compensation                                                 │
│ patch_description        │ The current Stage 3 reintegrates gated slopes as a pure integrator: any slope bias        │
│                          │ accumulates as drift, decoupling the output from true signal levels. This hurts lag_error │
│                          │ (0.42), correlation (0.83), and noise_reduction. The previous-generation variant that     │
│                          │ blended reintegrated slopes back toward the level-anchored Holt output scored better on   │
│                          │ lag while keeping slope churn low. This edit: (1) blends the reintegrated path with the   │
│                          │ Holt level path (0.7 hs / 0.3 reint) to prevent integrator drift, (2) widens the slope    │
│                          │ median filter to a 5-tap window for stronger inter-window slope consistency, and (3) adds │
│                          │ forward extrapolation along the Kalman slope estimate to compensate the smoothing group   │
│                          │ delay, reducing lag error. These directly target the multi-objective trade-off:           │
│                          │ slope-change suppression comes from the wider median, tracking accuracy comes from level  │
│                          │ anchoring, and responsiveness comes from lag compensation.                                │
