│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ kalman_rts_adaptive_smoother                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ A fundamentally different approach: instead of sliding-window regression, this runs a     │
│                          │ **local-linear Kalman filter over the entire signal** with **innovation-adaptive          │
│                          │ measurement/process noise**, followed by a **hysteresis trend-lock** on the slope         │
│                          │ estimate.                                                                                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Why this should perform better:                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Sequential Bayesian state estimation** replaces window averaging entirely — the      │
│                          │ level and slope are tracked as hidden states, so noise suppression accumulates over the   │
│                          │ whole history rather than a fixed W-sample window, while responsiveness is governed by    │
│                          │ the Kalman gain (adaptive, not window-size-locked).                                       │
│                          │ 2. **Innovation-adaptive R**: when innovations (prediction errors) spike, measurement     │
│                          │ noise is temporarily inflated — outliers are automatically down-weighted without a        │
│                          │ separate despike stage.                                                                   │
│                          │ 3. **Slope-state tracking with sign hysteresis**: the Kalman slope estimate is damped     │
│                          │ toward zero unless it exceeds a robust noise threshold, directly attacking the            │
│                          │ slope_changes / false_reversals metrics without adding lag to the level.                  │
│                          │ 4. Output is sampled causally at times t = W-1 … n-1, preserving the exact same output    │
│                          │ length and alignment as the original interface, with effectively zero group delay at each │
│                          │ output point.                                                                             │
