╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ cross                                                                                     │
│ patch_name               │ kalman_sg_trendlock_hybrid                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ Crossover strategy combining the strongest elements of the top-performing programs:       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **From the best-scoring program (SG-based, score 0.58)**: The Savitzky-Golay           │
│                          │ trend-preserving smoothing stage plus the recency-weighted sliding-window aggregation.    │
│                          │ This pipeline produced the lowest slope_changes (41.2) and false_reversals (36.6), which  │
│                          │ dominate the combined score. I keep this architecture but make the aggregation weights    │
│                          │ steeper (exp over [-2, 0] instead of [-1, 0]) to cut the lag error it suffered.           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **From the current/accuracy-strong programs (corr 0.88-0.93)**: The soft               │
│                          │ innovation-adaptive constant-velocity Kalman pre-filter (NIS capped at 12) that maintains │
│                          │ high correlation and tracking accuracy, replacing the cruder 1D Kalman of the SG program. │
│                          │ This should recover some of the accuracy (corr 0.78) the SG program lost.                 │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 3. **From the trend-lock program**: A trend-lock hysteresis post-process, here            │
│                          │ implemented directly on output differences: a slope reversal is only accepted if the      │
│                          │ opposing diff is significant relative to the local robust (MAD) noise scale of the diffs; │
│                          │ insignificant reversals are snapped to the local median trend. This directly attacks the  │
│                          │ slope-change and false-reversal metrics without adding phase delay (big reversals pass    │
│                          │ through untouched).                                                                       │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 4. **From the current program**: The edge-preserving 5-tap median snap to remove isolated │
│                          │ wiggles without attenuating ramps.                                                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The whole pipeline is vectorized (sliding_window_view matrix products, convolution, no    │
│                          │ per-window Python regression loops), so execution time stays low (~0.05s vs 0.13s of the  │
│                          │ loop-based versions), keeping the efficiency score at 1.0. A pure-NumPy SG fallback is    │
│                          │ included so the code runs even without SciPy. Expected outcome:                           │
│                          │ slope_changes/false_reversals near the SG program's best values while restoring           │
│                          │ correlation and lag via the better Kalman, zero-lag window-end evaluation, and the        │
│                          │ trend-lock snap.                                                                          │
