│ patch_name               │ trend_prediction_lag_compensation                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The metrics plateau at 0.62 across many filter tweaks: lag_error stuck at 0.41,           │
│                          │ responsiveness at 0.71, while noise metrics barely move. The bottleneck is the structural │
│                          │ phase/group delay of the SG + convolution pipeline, not the post-hoc snapping. A          │
│                          │ principled fix is predictive lag compensation: after smoothing, extrapolate the local     │
│                          │ quadratic trend forward by roughly the effective group delay (half the SG window + half   │
│                          │ the convolution window ≈ window_size samples). This is a classic zero-phase-lag technique │
│                          │ (equivalent to "smoothed prediction" / forward-stepping the SG polynomial fit). It        │
│                          │ directly targets lag_error and responsiveness without adding noise, since extrapolation   │
│                          │ uses the already-denoised, curvature-aware quadratic fit rather than raw data. Compute    │
│                          │ the slope (first difference) and half-acceleration (second difference) of x_smooth via    │
│                          │ vectorized gradients, then step y forward: y_pred = y + delay*(dy + 0.5*delay*d2y). Clip  │
│                          │ the correction by the local noise sigma to avoid over-shooting in flat noisy regions      │
│                          │ (safety cap), preserving reversal performance. This is O(n), adds negligible execution    │
│                          │ time, and leaves all proven snapping machinery intact.                                    │
