│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ slope_median_stabilizer                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ The metrics show the dominant weaknesses are slope changes (108.20) and false reversals   │
│                          │ (85.20) with smoothness at only 0.16, while lag (0.33) and responsiveness (0.75) are      │
│                          │ already good. The current binomial post-smoother is linear, so isolated single-sample     │
│                          │ slope sign flips survive convolution. A non-linear fix is needed: apply a 5-tap median    │
│                          │ filter to the first difference (slope estimate) of the output, then integrate back. This  │
│                          │ kills isolated slope sign reversals (the classic "spurious flip" pattern) with a          │
│                          │ non-linear operator while leaving sustained, genuine trend reversals untouched — unlike   │
│                          │ stronger linear smoothing, which would add phase lag and hurt lag_error/tracking. Since   │
│                          │ median of the derivative only alters samples where the derivative is an isolated outlier, │
│                          │ genuine dynamics pass through essentially unchanged, so accuracy and responsiveness are   │
│                          │ preserved. Implementation is O(n) and vectorized, keeping efficiency at 1.00. I apply it  │
│                          │ after the existing binomial pass, only when the output is long enough, and keep endpoints │
│                          │ safe by padding with edge-replicated differences.                                         │
