│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ joint_window_recency_tilt_2w7                                                             │
│ patch_description        │ Co-mutate the effective smoothing extent and recency tilt as a matched pair, per the      │
│                          │ recommendation: widen the regression window from W to 2W+7 (buying                        │
│                          │ noise-reduction/smoothness headroom that cut false reversals in Gen 42) while steepening  │
│                          │ the exponential recency tilt to e^-1.2 spacing per sample so the fit's effective center   │
│                          │ of mass stays anchored near the window end, recovering the lag that naive widening would  │
│                          │ cost. The wider window is used only for the local regression; the output-length contract  │
│                          │ is preserved by evaluating each fit at the most recent sample and slicing. To keep        │
│                          │ efficiency at 1.00, the per-window Huber reweighting is applied in a single vectorized    │
│                          │ MAD pass over a strided view instead of the previous multi-solve IRLS loop with a Python  │
│                          │ inner loop. The final regression value is computed by a fixed pre-factored                │
│                          │ normal-equation solve of the weight matrix (weights are constant across windows),         │
│                          │ reducing the per-window cost to a few dot products. This jointly targets                  │
│                          │ slope_changes/false_reversals (wider support + steeper tilt) and lag_error (tilt          │
│                          │ compensates), while keeping correlation intact since the quadratic fit evaluated at t=0   │
│                          │ remains zero-phase.                                                                       │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
