│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ despike_and_trendlock_stages                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The current program's weakest metrics are noise_reduction (0.33) and                      │
│                          │ slope_changes/false_reversals (41.0/36.4). The champion's successful history shows that   │
│                          │ structural *additions* to the cascade — not slope-domain rewrites — are what improve      │
│                          │ these metrics while keeping the 100% success rate. I propose two low-risk, fully          │
│                          │ vectorized, defensively-clamped stages added to the existing Kalman + Savitzky-Golay      │
│                          │ cascade:                                                                                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **ImpulseDespike (new Stage 0, before the Kalman filter):** A 5-tap running median     │
│                          │ with robust MAD-based thresholding removes impulse-like outliers before the Kalman filter │
│                          │ sees them. This directly improves noise_reduction (outliers inflate the MAD noise         │
│                          │ estimate and inject process noise through the NIS adaptation), and it costs O(n) with     │
│                          │ edge-clamped padding so short/edge inputs remain safe.                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **TrendLock slope damping (new Stage 3, after the existing median-snap pass):** The    │
│                          │ Kalman's velocity estimate gives us a smoothed slope proxy; EMA-smoothing the first       │
│                          │ difference of the output and nudging sub-noise steps toward it suppresses spurious        │
│                          │ directional reversals (targeting slope_changes and false_reversals) without adding group  │
│                          │ delay, since the nudge is proportional to slope disagreement, not a shift of the level.   │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Both stages are pure additions: they preserve the existing Kalman/SG convolution math,    │
│                          │ keep the output length convention (`n - W + 1`), and carry explicit small-input guards    │
│                          │ (`n < 5`, `len < 3`) mirroring the champion's defensive-clamp discipline. No slope-domain │
│                          │ rewriting, no endpoint evaluation changes, no parameter mutation of existing stages.      │
