│ patch_name               │ add_zero_phase_polish_stage                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The weakest metrics are noise_reduction (0.33), false_reversals (21.4), and slope_changes │
│                          │ (24.6). The champion cascade already achieves near-zero lag and perfect efficiency, so    │
│                          │ the safe way to attack the remaining noise gap is a *final zero-phase polishing stage*: a │
│                          │ small odd-length (7-tap) quadratic Savitzky-Golay pass applied to the Stage-5 output.     │
│                          │ Because SG is a symmetric, zero-phase FIR smoother, it adds **no group delay** —          │
│                          │ lag_error and responsiveness stay intact — while it further suppresses the residual       │
│                          │ high-frequency noise that survives the hysteresis snap. Its short window (7) preserves    │
│                          │ genuine trend dynamics and cannot introduce the endpoint/length contract issues that      │
│                          │ killed over-elaborate generations. Following the common-thread guidance, the new stage    │
│                          │ ships with the same defensive clamps as the champion's sg_win handling (odd-length clamp, │
│                          │ min 5, bounded by signal length), is purely additive at the end of the cascade (no        │
│                          │ slope-domain rewriting, no parameter mutation of existing stages), and keeps the length   │
│                          │ contract and O(n) cost (savgol is a fixed-tap convolution).                               │
