│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ aggressive_median_snap_wider_polish                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The weakest metric is noise_reduction (0.33) with smoothness at 0.45, while correlation   │
│                          │ (0.79) and lag (0.43) have headroom to trade. Rather than touching the co-swept           │
│                          │ window/tilt pair (known to interact destructively), I strengthen the zero-phase           │
│                          │ noise-attacking stages that add no group delay:                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. `_median_snap` (Stage 3.5): currently blends sub-noise samples 50/50 with the local    │
│                          │ median only when steps fall below 0.5σ. I make the blend graded — closer to the noise     │
│                          │ floor gets a stronger pull toward the median (up to 0.75), and raise the trigger          │
│                          │ threshold to 0.7σ. Median filtering is edge-preserving, so genuine monotonic trends       │
│                          │ (whose steps are systematic, not sub-noise relative to local σ) pass untouched; this      │
│                          │ specifically removes residual fine-scale jitter after the SG stage, boosting              │
│                          │ noise_reduction and smoothness without adding phase delay.                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. `ZeroPhasePolishStage` (Stage 6): widen the symmetric SG polish from 7 to 9 taps. A    │
│                          │ zero-phase FIR adds no lag; a quadratic fit over 9 taps still tracks the 0.5 Hz–5 Hz      │
│                          │ components of the test signal well, but attenuates residual high-frequency variance       │
│                          │ further, directly attacking noise_reduction and reducing spurious slope sign flips        │
│                          │ (false_reversals, slope_changes).                                                         │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Both edits are zero-phase, so lag_error and responsiveness_score should be unaffected     │
│                          │ while noise_reduction, smoothness_score, and reversal counts improve.                     │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
