│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ bidir_kalman_tautstring                                                                   │
│ patch_description        │ Fundamentally different paradigm: replace the lag-inducing "wide forward SG +             │
│                          │ recency-tilt compensation" architecture with **zero-phase bidirectional adaptive Kalman   │
│                          │ smoothing**. The prior design fights its own group delay: it widens the smoothing window  │
│                          │ (adding lag), then tries to claw lag back with an exponentially tilted window (adding     │
│                          │ bias and losing smoothness). Instead, I run the same capped-NIS adaptive                  │
│                          │ constant-velocity Kalman filter twice — once forward, once on the time-reversed signal —  │
│                          │ and average the two. This is the classic forward-backward (RTS-style) trick: the backward │
│                          │ pass contributes *negative* group delay that exactly cancels the forward pass's lag, so   │
│                          │ we get full smoothing-window noise suppression at near-zero phase. This directly attacks  │
│                          │ lag_error (0.47) and correlation (0.75) simultaneously, which the tilted-window approach  │
│                          │ could never do.                                                                           │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Second difference: the length contract (len(y) = n − W + 1) is met with a **symmetric     │
│                          │ (zero-phase) weighted window aggregation** instead of a recency-tilted one, so the        │
│                          │ contraction step itself adds no lag. Third, spurious reversals are attacked with a robust │
│                          │ **local taut-string-style diff shrinkage**: diffs that oppose the local median trend and  │
│                          │ are insignificant vs. local MAD are snapped to the local median slope, then re-integrated │
│                          │ and convex-blended — targeting slope_changes/false_reversals (the two heaviest weighted   │
│                          │ weaknesses) without an extra smoothing stage that would hurt responsiveness. Finally a    │
│                          │ 3-tap median (blended, always axis=1) kills isolated single-sample spikes at zero phase   │
│                          │ cost, per the validated recommendation.                                                   │
