│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ bidirectional_kalman_fusion                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The Kalman stage is the only causal (phase-lagging) element in the pipeline; the SG,      │
│                          │ median snap, and polish are all zero-phase. Prior-gen evidence (the crossover program)    │
│                          │ shows that fusing the forward capped-Q adaptive Kalman 50/50 with a time-reversed pass    │
│                          │ reduces slope_changes (23.2 vs 24.2), lag_error (0.42 vs 0.43), and false_reversals (20.4 │
│                          │ vs 21.0) while leaving correlation/smoothness unchanged — the backward pass sees turning  │
│                          │ points with opposite-signed phase error, so averaging cancels interior lag at zero        │
│                          │ smoothness cost. I add a thin BidirectionalKalmanStage wrapper around the untouched,      │
│                          │ validated AdaptiveKalmanStage and swap it into the pipeline run(), keeping the graded     │
│                          │ median snap, recency convolution, hysteresis snap, and polish intact. Short-input guard   │
│                          │ (n < 4) falls back to the single forward pass, so n = window_size and n = window_size+1   │
│                          │ edge cases remain safe.                                                                   │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
