│ patch_name               │ mad_noise_sigma_from_input                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ The noise sigma used by the two slope-consistency passes is currently estimated from      │
│                          │ `x_smooth`, which has already passed through two Kalman passes and two SG passes. By that │
│                          │ point the residual differences are tiny, so `sigma` is far below the true                 │
│                          │ measurement-noise scale, making the snap/lock thresholds too tight to catch noise-induced │
│                          │ slope churn (slope_changes=14.6, false_reversals=12.8). Estimating sigma robustly from    │
│                          │ the raw despiked input instead (MAD of first differences / sqrt(2)) yields a threshold    │
│                          │ calibrated to the actual noise level, so the median-snap and trend-lock passes can        │
│                          │ suppress a much larger fraction of spurious reversals without harming genuine trend       │
│                          │ changes (which still exceed the threshold). This directly attacks slope_changes and       │
│                          │ false_reversals while leaving lag and correlation intact.                                 │
